      "thm:exact-fixed-budget-frontier"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(S^*\\in\\arg\\max_{|S|\\le k}F(S)\\), which exists because the ground set is finite, and abbreviate \\(\\rho=\\rho_k(K,R)\\).  Let \\(S_i\\) be the population greedy set after \\(i\\) steps and put \\(G_i=\\mathrm{OPT}-F(S_i)\\).  If \\(G_i\\le0\\), monotonicity makes every desired lower bound immediate.  Otherwise, let \\(T_i=S^*\\setminus S_i\\).  If \\(T_i\\) were empty, monotonicity would give \\(F(S_i)\\ge F(S^*)\\), contrary to \\(G_i>0\\).  Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}exact\\text{-}fixed\\text{-}budget\\text{-}frontier}\\), Definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}submodularity\\text{-}ratio}\\), and monotonicity therefore give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n \\sum_{j\\in T_i}\\{F(S_i\\cup\\{j\\})-F(S_i)\\}\n &\\ge \\rho\\{F(S_i\\cup S^*)-F(S_i)\\}\\\\\n &\\ge \\rho\\{\\mathrm{OPT}-F(S_i)\\}=\\rho G_i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThere are at most \\(k\\) summands, so the greedy marginal is at least \\(\\rho G_i/k\\).  Hence\n\\[\n G_{i+1}\\le(1-\\rho/k)G_i.\n\\]\nSince \\(F(\\varnothing)=0\\), iteration for \\(i=0,\\ldots,k-1\\) yields\n\\[\n F(S_g)\\ge\\left\\{1-\\left(1-\\frac\\rho k\\right)^k\\right\\}\\mathrm{OPT}.\n\\]\nThe inequality \\(1-x\\le e^{-x}\\), applied with \\(x=\\rho/k\\in[0,1]\\), proves the second population bound.  This recurrence is the standard submodularity-ratio greedy argument; the model-specific input is the exact value of \\(\\rho\\).\n\nNow work on the stated uniform-error event and let \\(\\widehat S_i\\) be the empirical greedy path.  For every candidate \\(j\\notin\\widehat S_i\\), both \\(\\widehat S_i\\) and \\(\\widehat S_i\\cup\\{j\\}\\) have size at most \\(k\\), and\n\\[\n \\left|\\{\\widehat F(\\widehat S_i\\cup\\{j\\})-\\widehat F(\\widehat S_i)\\}\n -\\{F(\\widehat S_i\\cup\\{j\\})-F(\\widehat S_i)\\}\\right|\\le2\\epsilon.\n\\]\nMaximizing the estimated marginal can therefore lose at most \\(4\\epsilon\\) relative to the largest true marginal.  Repeating the preceding ratio argument gives\n\\[\n \\mathrm{OPT}-F(\\widehat S_{i+1})\n \\le\\left(1-\\frac\\rho k\\right)\n \\{\\mathrm{OPT}-F(\\widehat S_i)\\}+4\\epsilon.\n\\]\nAfter \\(k\\) iterations,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n F(\\widehat S)\n &\\ge\\left\\{1-\\left(1-\\frac\\rho k\\right)^k\\right\\}\\mathrm{OPT}\n -4\\epsilon\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}(1-\\rho/k)^i\\\\\n &\\ge\\left\\{1-\\left(1-\\frac\\rho k\\right)^k\\right\\}\\mathrm{OPT}-4k\\epsilon.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves the empirical claim.",
    "justification": "The replacement theorem translates the exact frontier into population and empirical selection performance.",
    "gap": "The recurrence is prior generic machinery; the new input is the exact Gaussian COT ratio.",
    "consumer": "A baseline-panel designer gets a strictly stronger small-budget factor and the same explicit estimation-error tolerance.",
